{
"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates how the regional political climate moderates the relationship between an individual's political conservatism and their COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "The present research examines political climate as a potential moderator of the association between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake.",
"page": 2
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate are associated with higher vaccine hesitancy and lower uptake, but the regional climate specifically moderates this relationship such that conservatives in liberal climates are less hesitant and more likely to get boosters than conservatives in conservative climates.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy... such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster... while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Taken together, our findings indicate that political climate... has a stronger association with vaccine and booster hesitancy for individuals who are higher on political conservatism as compared to those who are politically liberal.",
"page": 8
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The authors analyzed online survey data from 683 US participants collected in early 2023, measuring political conservatism, regional political climate via zip code election data, and vaccine/booster status and hesitancy.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided by participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times... We used this % Republican vote by zip code as a measure of conservative regional climate.",
"page": 3
},
{
"text": "Vaccine hesitancy... was measured using the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale... Booster hesitancy was assessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy.",
"page": 3
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "There is a significant interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate for vaccine hesitancy, indicating that the association between conservatism and hesitancy is stronger in conservative regions.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively)",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate. As political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals who live in a more conservative regional climate.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Liberals exhibit low vaccine hesitancy regardless of the political climate of their region, whereas conservatives' hesitancy varies significantly based on their environment.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "For the most politically liberal individuals, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regional climates.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Conservatives living in liberal political climates are significantly more likely to receive a COVID-19 booster than conservatives living in conservative political climates.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
"page": 8
},
{
"text": "as political conservatism increased, the likelihood of receiving a booster was lower for participants in a conservative regional climate as compared to those in a liberal regional climate.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Unlike hesitancy and booster status, the interaction between political conservatism and regional climate was not significant for initial vaccine status, likely due to high prevalence of mandates.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this difference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine mandates.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The authors state that the study cannot establish causal or directional associations due to its cross-sectional design, and that zip-code-based climate measures may be confounded by other regional factors like population density.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Finally, it should be noted that this data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
"page": 10
},
{
"text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The findings may not generalize to broader US populations because the sample only included White and Black participants recruited via an online platform, and self-reported data is susceptible to bias.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes participants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
"page": 10
},
{
"text": "Another limitation in this dataset is the reliance on self-reported vaccine status. Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"theoretical_positioning": [
{
"claim": "The authors position their work as extending previous research that focused on either political orientation or political climate in isolation by examining the interaction between the two.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact of both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has been little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local political climate.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "Previous studies have focused on either political orientation or political climate. By looking at political orientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more likely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"interlocutor_claim": [
{
"claim": "The paper disputes the sufficiency of previous research that only examined political orientation or political climate separately, arguing that the interaction between the two provides novel insights into booster uptake among conservatives.",
"quotes": [
